WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.780
In the first film, birds made one moth morph survive better than the other.

2
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:15.380
Now take a gall. If wasps attack small galls and birds attack large ones, which gall

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.380
is safest?

4
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:21.963
The goldenrod gall fly lays an egg in a bud of tall goldenrod.

5
00:00:23.203 --> 00:00:25.703
The plant grows a round gall around the larva.

6
00:00:26.903 --> 00:00:28.643
The larva spends the winter inside.

7
00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:34.463
The gall is made by insect and plant together, and galls vary widely in size.

8
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:39.567
Line the galls up by size, small on the left, large on the right.

9
00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.027
Above them we will draw relative fitness.

10
00:00:44.347 --> 00:00:49.067
That is how well larvae in galls of each size survive, compared with the average.

11
00:00:50.227 --> 00:00:53.387
A point above the dashed line means better than average.

12
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.200
From the left come the wasps.

13
00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.580
A parasitoid develops on or in a host and ultimately kills it.

14
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.080
This parasitoid wasp attacks larvae in small galls more than in large ones.

15
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.800
Many small galls go dark.

16
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.360
From the right come the birds.

17
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.680
Birds more often peck open the large galls and take the larva inside.

18
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.720
Many large galls go dark too.

19
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:26.703
So the middle survives best, with two named enemies pressing from opposite sides.

20
00:01:28.003 --> 00:01:31.223
Take a simple model where the two enemies are equally strong.

21
00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:36.683
The fitness curve rises, peaks over intermediate galls, and falls again.

22
00:01:38.203 --> 00:01:41.983
Favouring the middle over both extremes is called stabilizing selection.

23
00:01:42.963 --> 00:01:45.723
Below, the spread of gall sizes narrows.

24
00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:50.297
Now, in the model, make wasp attack heavier than bird attack.

25
00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:54.097
Does the average surviving gall stay in the middle?

26
00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:07.273
No. In the model, the peak slides toward larger galls, away from the stronger enemy.

27
00:02:08.593 --> 00:02:11.453
The spread still narrows, but the average moves up.

28
00:02:12.793 --> 00:02:15.393
Favouring one end like this is directional selection.

29
00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:20.953
So one trait can carry a stabilizing and a directional component at once.

30
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:27.167
A field study found this pattern in central Pennsylvania, across twenty fields over

31
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:32.887
two years. Parasitoid attack was more frequent than bird attack in those fields.

32
00:02:34.147 --> 00:02:38.747
Intermediate galls survived best, and selection also favoured larger galls.

33
00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.670
Now flip the hump into a dip.

34
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.410
The middle does worst, and both ends do better.

35
00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.870
That shape is called disruptive selection.

36
00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:55.030
Theory predicts it when individuals crowd onto the commonest resource, and those

37
00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.830
that use something different do better.

38
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.120
One end, the middle, or both ends.

39
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.360
Directional, stabilizing and disruptive are shapes of one curve.

40
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.480
They are not boxes a trait must fit.

41
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.020
So read the curve first, then name the mode.
